## 1. The idea in one paragraph

The monoculture literature says shared models correlate *outcomes* - a
static harm. This paper asks what monoculture does to ecosystems of models
that **retrain** on data their own deployments generate, and the answer is a
different kind of harm with different mathematics: shared models align the
*directions* of the learners' performative feedback, and aligned feedback
resonates. The systemic variable is not the number of firms but the
**effective number of independent models**, the leading eigenvalue of the
correlation matrix of the firms' performative responses. Fifty dealers
fine-tuning one vendor's foundation model are, dynamically, close to one very
large learner, and the market they share inherits that learner's instability.
Three consequences follow, and they are the paper. First, a **certification
gap**: every firm can pass an individual stability test while the ecosystem
fails a market-level one, so per-model evaluation is structurally
insufficient in a shared endogenous environment. Second, a **supply-chain
stability boundary** in (number of firms) x (shared-model fraction) that
turns foundation-model concentration from a talking point into a term in a
condition. Third, an **adoption game** in which monoculture is the Nash
destination of unregulated model choice, with a price of anarchy in stability
growing linearly in market size and two closed-form interventions. A
supervision result closes the loop: because `R` is unobservable to any
outsider, we give a statistic a regulator can compute from public prices
alone, and show it sharpens exactly as the ecosystem approaches the boundary.

## 2. Why this is a new framework, and why this venue wants it

**The framework.** `N` heterogeneous learners, each reshaping a shared
environment, coupled through spillover; stability, welfare, adoption
incentives, and observability all expressed through one object, the alignment
structure of the learners' response Jacobians. The three adjacent literatures
each lack a piece: monoculture (Kleinberg-Raghavan; Bommasani et al.) is
static and has no dynamics; multiplayer performative prediction (Brown et
al.; Narang et al.; Piliouras-Yu) has dynamics but homogeneous-coupling
conditions and no model-provenance structure, no adoption game, no
observability theory; the systemic-risk literature has spectra and early
warning but no learning micro-foundation. The related-work section proves the
deltas rather than asserting them - in particular against Narang et al.'s
heterogeneous decision-dependent games, whose conditions bound couplings by
magnitudes and are blind to direction: alignment is precisely the directional
information their sufficient conditions discard, and the paper exhibits an
ecosystem that their magnitude conditions call borderline that alignment
shows is stable, and vice versa. [PROVABLE - construct the two examples
explicitly]

**What the venue asked for.** EconML's Theme 2 ("Ecosystems with many
interacting models") lists, verbatim: *"Algorithmic monoculture and model
multiplicity"*, *"Feedback loops and performative prediction effects"*,
*"Market concentration among AI service providers"*, *"AI supply chains and
their dynamics"*, *"Multi-agent learning dynamics in economic environments"*,
and *"Ecosystem-level incentive design"*. One model hits all six. The paper
also carries the three things an economics-literate audience checks for and
ML submissions usually lack: an explicit equilibrium analysis, a welfare
statement (the instability externality and its price of anarchy), and an
identification argument (what an outside observer can and cannot infer, with
a placebo design on real data).

**Why still REFLEX-based.** The base project supplies the only setting we
know of where all of this is *falsifiable end-to-end*: a genuine `N`-dealer
simulator (bit-for-bit single-dealer reduction at `N = 1`), the homogeneous
special case already verified against theory (measured amplification
`1.74x / 3.16x` vs predicted `N_eff = 2 / 3` - the new theory must reproduce
these as its `R = 11'` column, an external validation checkpoint), closed-form
constants computed from microstructure primitives rather than fitted, and a
36-year real-data panel for the supervision leg.

## 3. Prior work this paper builds on and cites

Beyond the three literatures above, two prior papers are load-bearing and
both are cited by DOI. Under double-blind both are cited neutrally in the
third person, as any public preprint or published article would be; neither
is described as the authors' own work, and no phrasing of the form "our
earlier paper" appears anywhere in the submission.

**REFLEX** (*Reflexive Equilibrium Fixed-point Learning for Endogenous
eXchanges*, arXiv:2608.16155,
<https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2608.16155>) is the direct base. It derives
the performative retraining modulus `m = epsilon*beta/gamma` from
microstructure primitives rather than treating its constants as unobservable,
validates predicted against measured stability within 8% in simulation, proves
the multi-dealer case for *identical* dealers sharing one pool of informed
flow, and calibrates on 36 years of corporate-bond data where the stability
margin contracts roughly `4.3-4.4x` from calm to crisis. Everything this
paper does sits strictly above that: REFLEX's multi-dealer result is the
homogeneous corner `R = 1 1'` of the alignment matrix, and it carries no
alignment object, no supply chain, no adoption game, no welfare statement and
no observability result. The relationship is stated plainly in related work,
and REFLEX's published `1.74x / 3.16x` amplification numbers are used as an
external validation checkpoint the new theory must reproduce.

**PEBSA** (*Predicting Economic Behavior via Sentiment Analysis*, IJECS
13(12), 2024, <https://doi.org/10.18535/ijecs/v13i12.4950>) is cited once,
briefly, and for a real reason rather than for completeness. It belongs to
the same broad question this paper's supervision result asks - what can be
inferred about the state of an economy from aggregate public signals that no
single participant controls - and the contrast is the point. PEBSA's
measurement channel is *exogenous*: sentiment is a signal about an economy the
observer stands outside of, and the accuracy of the inference does not depend
on the economy's own dynamics. The observable in this paper is *endogenous*:
the public prices a supervisor reads are generated by the very adaptive agents
being monitored, so the measurement channel is part of the system, and the
striking consequence is that the signal gets *stronger* as the system nears
instability. One sentence in related work, framed as exogenous-signal versus
endogenous-signal macro inference. Do not oversell the link; the value is the
contrast, not a shared method.

## 4. Model

`N` firms, each deploying a learned policy over a `d`-dimensional decision.
Firm `i` has objective curvature `Gamma_i` (SPD) and response Jacobian
`E_i in R^{d x d}` - how its own deployment reshapes the flow it faces.
Spillover `kappa in [0,1]` routes each firm's response into competitors'
environments. Joint retraining Jacobian `J` with blocks
`J_ii = -Gamma_i^{-1} beta E_i`, `J_ij = -kappa Gamma_i^{-1} beta E_j`.

Alignment objects: pairwise `r_ij = <vec E_i, vec E_j> / (||E_i||_F
||E_j||_F)`; alignment matrix `R = (r_ij)`; **effective number of
independent models** `lambda_max(R) in [1, N]`. Supply chain:
`E_i = sqrt(s) E_shared + sqrt(1-s) Xi_i` with independent idiosyncratic
parts; `s` = shared-foundation-model fraction.

**Naming, and the defensive footnote.** `lambda_max(R)` is called the
effective number of independent models, but the paper does not present the
*quantity* as new - only its role. Spectral summaries of a correlation matrix
have a long history as effective counts: the participation ratio and inverse
participation ratio in physics, effective rank in signal processing
(Roy-Vetterli), and the leading-eigenvalue market mode in the
random-matrix-theory finance literature (Laloux et al.; Plerou et al.), where
`lambda_max` of a return correlation matrix is *already* read as the strength
of the common factor. Saying so costs one footnote and buys two things: it
disarms the cheapest available referee objection ("you renamed effective
rank"), and it lets the paper claim the thing that actually is new, which is
that this familiar statistic, computed on **response Jacobians rather than
returns**, is the argument of a stability condition. The novelty is the
object it is computed from and the theorem it enters, and the paper says
exactly that.

**Market shares.** Firms are not equal-sized, and in an AI supply chain they
are conspicuously unequal - a vendor's customer list runs from one bulge
bracket desk to fifty small ones. Firm `i` intermediates share `w_i` of flow,
`sum_i w_i = 1`, which scales how much of the shared environment its
deployment actually moves. The object that governs stability is then the
**share-weighted alignment matrix**

```
   R_w = W^{1/2} R W^{1/2} ,        W = diag(N w_i) ,
```

normalized so `w_i = 1/N` for all `i` recovers `R` exactly. Two consequences
worth a paragraph each in the paper, and both are cheap to prove and to
simulate. (i) `lambda_max(R_w) >= lambda_max(R)` under concentration in
shares: **share concentration and model concentration compound**, so a market
where the large firms are also the ones sharing a vendor is strictly more
fragile than either measure alone suggests. (ii) The relevant policy quantity
is a *share-weighted* diversity index; a headcount-based one (regulate by
number of firms on each model) is defeated by the same clustered topology
that defeats mean alignment. This is the economically meaningful direction of
heterogeneity for this audience, and it is the direction the earlier draft
did not have.

Three equilibrium concepts, kept typographically distinct throughout (this
audience checks): the PSNE of the quoting game; the Nash equilibrium of the
*adoption* game; the social optimum. The single-firm performatively stable
point is notation, not a fourth concept, and is introduced as such to save a
column inch.

## 5. The results

Ordered by economic interest. Results 1-5 are the 9-page body. The general
spectral theory that used to open this section is now Section 5.6, stated in
the body and proved in the appendix, because its interest is computational
and this is not a computational venue.

### Result 1 - The alignment identity and the supply-chain boundary [VERIFIED anchors / PROVABLE]

Equal moduli `m_i = m`, heterogeneous response *directions*. The joint
spectral radius is exactly

```
   rho(J) = m ( 1 + kappa ( lambda_max(R) - 1 ) ) ,
```

so the ecosystem is stable iff
`epsilon < (gamma/beta) / (1 + kappa (lambda_max(R) - 1))`. Three anchors,
each checked independently: monoculture (`R = 11'`) recovers
`N_eff = 1 + kappa(N-1)` and the published `1.74x / 3.16x` measurements;
orthogonal responses give `rho = m`, so a hundred firms perturbing the
environment in a hundred orthogonal directions are dynamically one firm; and
maximal diversity (simplex responses) gives `1 - kappa`, independently
matching the differential-mode eigenvalue the homogeneous theory derives by a
completely different route. Two derivations landing on one number is the kind
of consistency the base project's verification layer exists to certify.
[VERIFIED anchors]

Under the supply-chain decomposition the alignments concentrate at
`r_ij -> s`, so `lambda_max(R) -> 1 + s(N-1)` and the boundary becomes

```
   epsilon  <  gamma / ( beta ( 1 + kappa s (N - 1) ) ) .
```

**The effective number of learners is the number of independent models, not
the number of firms.** Fifty dealers fine-tuning one vendor's model are
dynamically `~ 1 + 49 kappa` learners at `s ~ 1` and barely more than one at
`s ~ 0`. The `(N, s)` phase diagram is the paper's central figure, and it is
the sentence the abstract is built around: foundation-model concentration
becomes a term in a stability condition rather than a talking point. The
`O(1/d)` correction to the concentration is stated; the finite-size
random-matrix refinement is **deliberately cut** and noted as the journal
extension, because an error band on a phase diagram is not what this audience
is reading for and it was the most expensive `[HARD]` item per page of
interest it bought.

Share-weighted version: substituting `R_w` for `R` throughout gives the same
identity with `lambda_max(R_w)`, and the compounding statement of Section 4
follows immediately. [PROVABLE]

### Result 2 - The certification gap: private stability does not imply systemic stability [PROVABLE]

This is the paper's headline claim in words, and the reason a non-technical
reader should care. Two questions that sound like one:

> Is this model stable when deployed?
> Is the ecosystem of models stable?

They come apart, and Result 1 says by exactly how much. Every firm can
satisfy its own condition `m_i < 1` while `rho(J) > 1`, because the
individual condition prices only a firm's effect on its own environment and
none of its effect on everyone else's. In the monoculture corner this has a
single memorable number: with full spillover and identical models the
ecosystem crosses the boundary at a **critical population**

```
   N_c  =  1 / m_1 ,
```

so a market of learners each certified at, say, `m_1 = 0.2` is fragile from
the fifth entrant onward, with no firm having done anything wrong. Away from
that corner `N_c` generalizes to the `(N, s, kappa)` surface of Result 1, and
the paper presents the scalar version first precisely because it is the
version a policymaker repeats.

**Why the naive diversity index fails.** Two ways, both with a constructed
counterexample and both stated as rules the paper follows. (i) *Mean
alignment understates clustered alignment*: three tightly-aligned firms among
ten otherwise-orthogonal ones destabilize a subspace the average barely
registers. The vendor-with-a-plurality topology, not the monopoly, is the
realistic case, and it gets its own experimental panel. (ii) *Headcount
understates share concentration*: by Section 4's `R_w`, the same alignment
pattern is more dangerous when the aligned firms are the large ones. Rule:
**all stability claims use the spectral form of the share-weighted matrix,
never a mean or a count.**

**Honest scope line.** The exact identity is proved for equal moduli. With
heterogeneous `m_i` the tempting mean-modulus formula is **provably false** -
orthogonal responses decouple the firms entirely, giving `max_i m_i`, not the
mean. The correct object is the modulus-weighted alignment matrix
`M^{1/2} R M^{1/2}` (`M = diag(m_i)`), which yields two-sided bounds, exact
in the equal-moduli and `kappa = 0` limits, with tightness measured against
modulus dispersion in simulation rather than asserted.

### Result 3 - The adoption game: monoculture as the Nash destination [PROVABLE]

Why would an ecosystem end up at high `s`? Because every private incentive
points there. Model provider choice as a population game: adopting the
market-leading model yields private quality gain `q`; one firm's adoption
moves ecosystem alignment by `O(1/N)`, so the private share of the
instability cost a firm creates vanishes as the market grows while `q` does
not. A textbook externality, and the paper says so plainly rather than
dressing it up. Results: (a) the game admits a potential, and Nash
equilibrium is full monoculture whenever `q` exceeds a threshold
**decreasing in `N`**, so bigger markets tip on smaller quality edges; (b)
the social optimum keeps interior diversity `s* < 1`, characterized by
marginal quality equal to marginal instability; (c) the **price of anarchy in
stability** is `(1 + kappa(N-1)) / (1 + kappa s* (N-1))`, growing linearly in
`N`; (d) the minimal restoring interventions in closed form (Result 4).

This discharges the CFP's "ecosystem-level incentive design" bullet with an
actual mechanism, and it supplies the paper's most important qualitative
claim: the unstable configuration is not an edge case to be guarded against
but the *destination* of unregulated adoption dynamics.

### Result 4 - Two interventions, and which one is available to whom [PROVABLE]

The paper deliberately carries two levers, because they act on different
parts of the mechanism and are available to different actors.

**Diversity floor / Pigouvian fee (the regulator's lever).** From Result 3,
the minimal intervention restoring `s <= s*` in closed form: either a floor
on the shared-model fraction, or a per-firm fee on shared-model adoption set
equal to the externalized marginal instability cost. Standard, correct, and
what an economics referee expects to see. It is also slow, requires a
supervisor who can observe model provenance, and is the harder sell.

**Retraining cadence (the operational lever).** The spillover `kappa` and the
per-step modulus are not fixed constants; they are functions of how often
firms refit relative to how fast the shared environment mixes. Slowing or
desynchronizing retraining shrinks the effective coupling, moving the
ecosystem inside the boundary *without* changing anyone's model, market
share, or vendor. This lever is already implemented and characterized in the
base project for the single-firm case, so it is nearly free here, and it is
the only intervention a firm or an exchange can deploy unilaterally and
immediately. The paper states the cadence-vs-stability tradeoff and notes the
cost side honestly: slower retraining means staler models, so this buys
stability with accuracy rather than for free.

The framing that ties them together, and the sentence to end the policy
section on: **both levers act on the interaction structure, not on any
individual learner's predictive quality.** No amount of making each model
better fixes an externality.

### Result 5 - Supervision from public prices [HARD - consistency at the workshop]

Everything above involves `R`, which no outsider observes; supervisors do not
get to inspect firms' models. But near the boundary the system tells on
itself. As `rho(J) -> 1` the *aligned* combination of firms' decisions
exhibits critical slowing down: the leading principal component of public
quotes has lag-1 autocorrelation tending to one and variance share growing
like `1/(1 - rho)`. We give the estimator (PC1 extraction plus AR
coefficient) and prove **consistency** for the distance `1 - rho` under the
linearized dynamics, with the rate improving as the boundary nears. The
near-unit-root superconsistency phenomenon means **supervision is easiest
exactly where it matters most**. The supervisor needs no access to any firm's
model, data, or code.

Workshop scope: consistency plus simulation in the body; the CLT, the
detection sample complexity for the composite test "certified stable vs not",
and the full near-unit-root treatment are appendix-and-journal. Stated
plainly: the asymptotics are classical, the object they are applied to is
new, and the paper claims only the second.

### 5.6 The general spectral theory [HARD - stated in the body, proved in the appendix]

Both of these were the previous draft's headline theorems. They are correct
and worth doing, but their interest is machinery, so they are stated in half
a column each with proof sketches and carried in full by the appendix, which
doubles as the journal draft.

**Exact reduction for arbitrary heterogeneous ecosystems.** `J` is
block-diagonal plus a spillover term of block-rank `d`, so its spectrum
reduces exactly to the roots of a `d x d` secular equation

```
   det( I_d  +  kappa * sum_i  E_i ( lambda I + (1-kappa) Gamma_i^{-1} beta E_i )^{-1} Gamma_i^{-1} beta )  =  0
```

plus the block-diagonal spectra, computable in `O(N d^3)` rather than
`O((Nd)^3)` by a block matrix determinant lemma. This is what makes Result 1
an instance of a general characterization rather than an isolated identity,
and that role is why it stays in the body at all. Fallback if the proof
resists: ship it verified to machine precision against dense eigensolves as a
numerical certificate, worded as such.

**Global convergence beyond linearization.** If Result 1's condition holds
uniformly over the operating region rather than only at the equilibrium, the
joint best-response map is a contraction in an explicit `Gamma`-weighted
norm: a unique PSNE exists and simultaneous retraining converges to it
globally at the rate the spectrum names. The contraction half is Banach and
low-risk, and it is the half that ships. The converse (linearized
`rho(J) > 1` implies divergence from generic initial conditions, via a
center-stable-manifold argument) is stated and deferred. Half a column here
answers "your whole theory is a linearization" before a referee has to ask.

## 6. The experiments

All in the base project's genuine `N`-dealer simulator; CPU, deterministic,
certified. Each maps to exactly one result, and the mapping is stated in the
paper so a referee can check coverage at a glance.

1. **The `(N, s)` phase diagram** (Result 1) - measured joint modulus over
   firms x shared-model fraction with the boundary overlaid. The `s = 1`
   column must reproduce the published `1.74x / 3.16x` numbers: an external
   validation checkpoint against a prior curated run, not a self-consistency
   check.
2. **Diversity buys stability** (Result 1) - fix `N` and the feedback gain
   beyond the monoculture boundary, then decorrelate the responses. Same
   market, same feedback strength, same number of firms: divergent at high
   alignment, convergent at low. The single most persuasive dynamic figure in
   the paper and the one that should be on the poster.
3. **The two ways diversity indices fail** (Result 2) - the clustered-vendor
   topology (three aligned firms among ten) going unstable at mean-alignment
   values a naive index calls safe, and the share-weighted companion where
   the same alignment pattern is safe among small firms and unstable among
   large ones. Reports `lambda_max(R_w)` against mean alignment and against
   headcount on the same axes.
4. **The adoption game** (Result 3) - Nash versus socially optimal `s` across
   quality gaps; price of anarchy against `N`.
5. **The two levers at matched cost** (Result 4) - the diversity floor and
   the retraining-cadence adjustment applied to the same unstable ecosystem,
   plotted as stability restored per unit of accuracy or quality given up.
   Directly reuses the base project's existing cadence machinery, which is
   what makes it affordable this late in the build. This is the panel that
   makes the policy section concrete rather than rhetorical.
6. **Real-data supervision** (Result 5) - PC1 variance share and lag-1
   autocorrelation of cross-sectional spread co-movement on the 212-CUSIP
   panel, 1990-2026, overlaid on the model-implied fragility index (which
   contracts roughly `4.3-4.4x` from calm to crisis in the base project's
   calibration); **placebo** on Treasury/macro series where no dealer-model
   channel exists. Framed as consistency evidence, never identification:
   co-movement is macro-contaminated and the data is not trade-level TRACE
   (provenance caveat carried verbatim from the base project).

**Cut from the previous plan:** the secular-reduction certification panel
(random heterogeneous `(Gamma_i, E_i)` versus dense eigensolves). It is a
correctness check on appendix machinery, it persuades nobody at this venue,
and it moves to the appendix as a table rather than a figure.

## 6. The workshop cut vs the full paper

**The 9-page body (Aug 29)** carries the full arc - it is a long-format
venue: framework + model; Theorem 1 (a stated with the reduction displayed, b
in full, c with the counterexample); Theorem 2 stated with proof sketch;
Theorems 3-4 in full; Theorem 5 estimator + consistency (CLT to appendix);
experiments 1, 2, 4, 5; policy and limitations. **Appendix:** all proofs;
experiment 3; the Narang-delta examples; the placebo; certificates. **The
main-track / journal version** (NeurIPS 2027 or an economics/finance
journal): completes Theorem 2's converse in full generality, Theorem 3's RMT
refinement, Theorem 5's detection theory, and adds the E3 program's
endogenous-participation extension. Nothing is claimed at the workshop
beyond what is proved or verified at submission; [HARD] items ship with
partial results labeled as such.

**Anonymization checklist (double-blind).** No author block; no repo URL
anywhere; the base project cited in third person ("a concurrent submission
derives the homogeneous boundary..."); scrubbed PDF metadata and figure
paths; simulator described generically with an anonymized-artifact promise.

## 7. Honest limitations

Theorem 2's uniform condition is checkable but conservative; Theorem 5's
asymptotics are classical (the object is new, the rates are not - said
plainly); the real-data leg is consistency-plus-placebo, not identification
of monoculture; alignment `R` is unobservable directly - Theorem 5 exists
precisely because of that, and the limitation section says so rather than
hiding it.

